decisions:
  - id: canvas-craftjs
    decision: Use Craft.js instead of tldraw or GrapesJS
    rationale: Craft.js renders actual DOM/CSS (not canvas approximations), MIT licensed, gives maximum control over UI since we build all panels ourselves. tldraw is great for diagrams but fights precise CSS layout. GrapesJS was rejected by user in favor of full control.
  - id: canvas-file-format
    decision: '*.canvas YAML files in project root, not .paradigm/canvas/'
    rationale: User wants canvas files to be first-class project objects that get committed to git, not hidden in .paradigm/. YAML is consistent with rest of Paradigm. Glob discovery supports flexible placement.
  - id: canvas-structured-layout
    decision: Structured CSS layout (flexbox/grid) not absolute positioning
    rationale: The killer feature is that designs output production-ready CSS. Absolute positioning produces unusable CSS. Structured layout means what you design is what ships.
  - id: canvas-graph-coexist
    decision: Canvas and Graph sections coexist as distinct tools
    rationale: Graph is the quick auto-generated topology view (symbol wiring diagram). Canvas is the persistent freeform design workspace. Different questions answered.
learnings:
  - Craft.js @0.2.x works cleanly with React 18 and Vite — no special config needed, ~40KB gzipped
  - Canvas section builds to 31.7KB gzipped with lazy loading — comparable to other sections, no bundle bloat
  - Craft.js useNode() hook provides clean read/write access to node props — setProp() callback pattern works well for property panels
  - File glob discovery for *.canvas files needs explicit ignore patterns (node_modules, .git, dist) to avoid slow walks on large projects
